    "Transpiration pulls the water column up through continuous xylem under tension — the cohesion-tension mechanism. Over 90% of the water a tree takes up is transpired, not used in photosynthesis.",
    "Trees do not heal. They compartmentalize — they wall off damage and grow new wood around it. Decayed wood never becomes sound again.",
    "CODIT describes four walls. Wall 1 resists spread up and down the stem and is the weakest. Wall 2 resists inward spread toward the center. Wall 3 resists lateral spread and is formed by ray cells. Wall 4, the barrier zone laid down by the cambium after wounding, is the strongest and separates all wood present at the time of injury from every ring formed afterward.",
    "Apical dominance: auxin produced at the leader suppresses lateral buds below it. Remove the leader and you release those buds — which is exactly why topping produces a dense, weakly attached mess of sprouts.",
    "Reaction wood restores orientation after a lean. Conifers make compression wood on the lower side and push. Hardwoods make tension wood on the upper side and pull.",
    "Most fine absorbing roots live in the top 6–12 inches of soil, where oxygen is. Roots need oxygen as much as water — this is why compaction and overwatering both kill trees, and why they look alike."
