/* ---------------- Flashcards ---------------- */
window.TTC_STUDY.flashcards = [
 ["biology","Which tissue moves water and dissolved minerals upward, and which moves manufactured sugars?","Xylem moves water and minerals upward from the roots. Phloem moves photosynthate in both directions, wherever energy is needed — including down to the root system."],
 ["biology","Where does a tree grow in length, and where does it grow in girth?","Length comes from apical meristems at the shoot and root tips (primary growth). Girth comes from the vascular cambium (secondary growth). A mark on the trunk never rises."],
 ["biology","Name the four CODIT walls in order of strength.","Wall 4, the barrier zone laid down after wounding, is strongest. Then Wall 3 (ray cells, resisting lateral spread), Wall 2 (annual ring boundary, resisting inward spread), and Wall 1 (resisting vertical spread) is weakest."],
 ["biology","Why does girdling the cambium kill everything above the injury?","The cambium generates both conducting tissues. Sever it all the way around and the phloem can no longer carry sugars down to the roots, so the root system starves and the top follows."],
 ["biology","What is apical dominance and why does topping defeat it?","Auxin from the terminal leader suppresses lateral buds. Removing the leader removes the suppression, releasing a dense flush of weakly attached epicormic sprouts."],
 ["biology","Compression wood versus tension wood — which tree, which side?","Conifers form compression wood on the underside and push the stem upright. Hardwoods form tension wood on the upper side and pull."],
 ["biology","Roughly what proportion of absorbed water is transpired rather than used in photosynthesis?","Over 90%. Transpiration is the engine that pulls the water column up, and photosynthesis consumes a very small fraction of the flow."],
 ["biology","What do mycorrhizae do for a tree?","Fungal hyphae extend far beyond the root surface and dramatically increase water and phosphorus uptake, in exchange for sugars from the tree."],
 ["biology","Sapwood or heartwood — which conducts water?","Sapwood. Heartwood is non-conducting; it provides structural support and is often the first tissue lost to decay."],

 ["soil","What is the ideal volumetric composition of a soil for root growth?","About 45% mineral, 5% organic matter and 50% pore space, with the pore space roughly half air and half water."],
 ["soil","What is the difference between soil texture and soil structure?","Texture is the sand-silt-clay proportion and cannot be changed in place. Structure is how those particles aggregate, and it is destroyed by compaction."],
 ["soil","Why does a red maple go chlorotic on limestone fill even though the soil contains plenty of iron?","Above roughly pH 7.5 iron converts to forms the tree cannot absorb. It is an availability problem, not a quantity problem — which is why the fix is pH management, not more iron."],
 ["soil","What soil pH range makes the broadest set of nutrients available?","Roughly 6.0 to 6.5 for most landscape trees."],
 ["soil","What does bulk density measure, and at what point do roots stop penetrating?","It measures compaction as dry mass per unit volume. Root penetration is generally restricted above about 1.4–1.6 g/cm³ in clay loam and 1.6–1.8 g/cm³ in sands."],
 ["soil","Define field capacity and permanent wilting point.","Field capacity is the water held after gravity drainage stops. Permanent wilting point is the moisture level below which the tree cannot extract water. The difference is plant-available water."],
 ["soil","What is CEC and why do sandy soils need different fertilization?","Cation exchange capacity is the soil's ability to hold nutrient cations. Sand has low CEC, so nutrients leach quickly — feed lighter and more often."],
 ["soil","Name the highest-return soil treatment in arboriculture.","Organic mulch, 2–4 inches deep, over as much of the root zone as possible, held 3–6 inches off the trunk."],

 ["id-selection","Which temperate genera have opposite branching?","Maple, ash, dogwood, the Caprifoliaceae, and horsechestnut — 'MAD Cap Horse'. Almost everything else is alternate."],
 ["id-selection","State the 10-20-30 diversity guideline.","No more than 10% of a tree population in a single species, 20% in a single genus, or 30% in a single family."],
 ["id-selection","What is the most consequential specification error in tree selection?","Mature size against the available space, above ground and below. It is what produces a lifetime of topping under utility lines and a slow decline in an undersized pit."],
 ["id-selection","What should you look for when inspecting nursery stock?","A single dominant leader, well-spaced scaffold branches, a visible root flare at the soil surface, and no circling, kinked or girdling roots in the ball."],
 ["id-selection","Roughly how much soil volume does a mature crown need?","A common working figure is about 2 cubic feet of rootable soil per square foot of projected mature crown area."],
 ["id-selection","Is 'native' sufficient justification for a species recommendation?","No. Natives support far more local wildlife, but boxelder and silver maple are native and both are poor urban choices. Site fitness still governs."],

 ["installation","How wide and how deep should a planting hole be?","Two to three times the width of the root ball, and no deeper than the ball. Roots need lateral room, not depth."],
 ["installation","What is the most common fatal planting error?","Planting too deep. It causes stem-girdling roots and basal decay, and the decline shows up five to fifteen years later when nobody connects it back to planting."],
 ["installation","Should you amend the backfill?","Generally no. Use the native soil from the hole. Heavy amendment creates a comfortable pocket that roots circle in rather than moving out into surrounding soil."],
 ["installation","How much water does a newly planted tree need per irrigation?","Roughly 1 to 1.5 gallons per inch of trunk caliper, applied to the root ball, deeply and infrequently."],
 ["installation","How long is the establishment period?","About one year per inch of trunk caliper in temperate climates. A 3-inch caliper tree stays on irrigation roughly three growing seasons."],
 ["installation","When should a newly planted tree be staked, and for how long?","Only when it cannot stand on its own or the site is very exposed. Attach low and loosely so the trunk can flex, and remove all hardware within one year."],
 ["installation","Should you fertilize at planting?","Not as a routine. There is no root system to take it up, and nitrogen forces shoot growth the roots cannot support. Correct deficiencies a soil test actually identified."],

 ["pruning","Name the three basic pruning cuts.","Removal cut (branch back to its parent at the collar), reduction cut (stem back to a lateral at least 1/3 the diameter of the stem removed), and heading cut (to a stub or undersized lateral — a defect outside of specific specialist practice)."],
 ["pruning","Where exactly does the final cut go?","Just outside the branch collar and branch bark ridge, never flush to the trunk. Flush cutting removes the collar and destroys the tree's ability to form the barrier zone."],
 ["pruning","Describe the three-cut method.","An undercut on the underside of the limb, a relief cut slightly beyond it to drop the weight, and a final cut at the collar. It prevents bark tearing down the trunk."],
 ["pruning","How much live foliage can be removed in a season?","Up to about 25% on a young, vigorous tree. Far less on a mature tree — often 10% or under — and close to nothing on an old, stressed or declining tree."],
 ["pruning","Why is topping malpractice rather than merely bad practice?","It removes the food-producing canopy, opens wounds too large to compartmentalize, and releases a mass of weakly attached epicormic sprouts — producing a more hazardous, shorter-lived tree at higher long-term cost."],
 ["pruning","What is lion's tailing and what does it do?","Stripping interior foliage and leaving tufts at the branch ends. It shifts weight outward, increases wind loading, exposes bark to sunscald, and reduces taper development."],
 ["pruning","List the ANSI A300 pruning types.","Cleaning, thinning, raising, reduction, and restoration."],
 ["pruning","When should you not prune an oak, and why?","Roughly April through July in oak wilt regions. Fresh wounds attract the sap beetles that vector the pathogen. Where a cut is unavoidable, seal it immediately."],
 ["pruning","Do wound dressings help?","No. They do not speed closure or prevent decay, and they are not recommended. The single accepted exception is sealing fresh oak wounds in oak wilt regions to deter beetle vectors."],
 ["pruning","What is a subordination cut for?","Reducing a competing stem so the intended leader stays dominant — the core move in young-tree structural pruning."],

 ["diagnosis","What is the difference between a sign and a symptom?","A sign is evidence of the causal organism itself — a conk, mycelium, frass, the insect. A symptom is the tree's response — wilting, chlorosis, dieback."],
 ["diagnosis","Damage appears uniformly across many species on a site. Biotic or abiotic?","Almost certainly abiotic. Pathogens and insects are host-specific and produce scattered, progressive patterns; herbicide, salt, gas, grade change and drought hit everything at once."],
 ["diagnosis","What proportion of urban tree problems are abiotic?","The majority. Construction damage, deep planting, compaction, drought, salt and girdling roots account for far more decline than insects and disease, which are frequently secondary."],
 ["diagnosis","What should you excavate before finalizing a decline diagnosis?","The root flare. Girdling roots, planting depth and root collar decay are invisible from the canopy and are among the most common true causes."],
 ["diagnosis","State the disease triangle.","Disease requires a susceptible host, a virulent pathogen, and a favorable environment. Remove any one side and disease does not develop."],
 ["diagnosis","In what order does IPM apply controls?","Monitor and identify, set an action threshold, then cultural, mechanical, biological, and chemical last and most narrowly targeted."],
 ["diagnosis","A tree has borers. What is the real question?","Why the tree was stressed enough to be attacked. Most borers and canker fungi are secondary — treating them without correcting the underlying stress buys one season."],

 ["risk","What three components determine a tree risk rating?","Likelihood of failure, likelihood of impacting a target, and consequences of that impact."],
 ["risk","Name the four likelihood-of-failure categories.","Improbable, possible, probable, and imminent — always stated for a defined time frame and specified conditions."],
 ["risk","What are the four risk ratings?","Low, moderate, high, and extreme."],
 ["risk","Distinguish Level 1, 2 and 3 assessments.","Level 1 is a limited visual, often a rapid pass over a population. Level 2 is a basic assessment — a systematic 360-degree ground inspection with mallet and probe. Level 3 is advanced, using tools like resistance drilling or tomography to answer a specific question."],
 ["risk","Why must a risk assessment state a time frame?","A rating is only meaningful for a defined period. Trees and targets change, and a rating without a time frame is not defensible."],
 ["risk","A large dead limb hangs over an unused field corner. Is this high risk?","No. Likelihood of failure may be high, but likelihood of impacting a target is very low and consequences are negligible, so the risk rating is low. Risk is not the same as defect."],
 ["risk","Name mitigation options short of removal.","End-weight reduction pruning, cabling or bracing, moving or restricting the target, restricting access, and increasing inspection frequency."],
 ["risk","What is residual risk?","The risk remaining after mitigation. It is never zero, and it belongs in the report."],

 ["safety","Which standard governs arboricultural safety in the US?","ANSI Z133 — Safety Requirements for Arboricultural Operations."],
 ["safety","How close may a non-qualified worker come to an energized conductor up to 50 kV?","No closer than 10 feet, with additional clearance required at higher voltages. Only qualified line-clearance arborists and their trainees may work inside the minimum approach distance."],
 ["safety","What is required before a climber makes a chain saw cut aloft?","Being tied in on two separate points of attachment, or otherwise secured as the standard requires."],
 ["safety","What belongs in a job briefing?","The work plan, identified hazards, assignments, emergency procedures, and the route to the nearest medical facility — repeated whenever conditions change."],
 ["safety","Name three chain saw rules for ground operation.","Never above shoulder height, never from a ladder, and never one-handed as routine practice — plus required leg, head, eye and hearing protection."],
 ["safety","What is the rule about the drop zone?","No personnel in it while cutting is in progress, with constant communication between climber and ground crew."],
 ["safety","Why is aerial rescue capability mandatory rather than optional?","A suspended, injured climber can deteriorate within minutes. Someone on every job site must be trained and equipped to bring them down."],

 ["water","Deep and infrequent, or light and daily?","Deep and infrequent. Frequent shallow watering builds a shallow root system that cannot survive a missed week or a hot spell."],
 ["water","Where do you water a newly planted tree?","On the root ball itself for the first year or two — it dries out faster than the surrounding backfill."],
 ["water","Why do overwatering and drought look the same?","Both end in root dysfunction. Saturated soil suffocates roots, so the tree cannot take up water and wilts exactly as a drought-stressed tree does. Dig and check before adding water."],
 ["water","When is the best time of day to irrigate?","Early morning. Evening watering leaves foliage wet overnight and favors foliar disease; midday loses more to evaporation."],
 ["water","What is winter desiccation?","Evergreen foliage drying out because frozen soil prevents replacement of transpired water. It is a water problem, not a cold problem — fall watering before freeze-up is the prevention."],
 ["water","How long after a drought does damage appear?","Often one to two growing seasons later, as dieback and decline. The connection is routinely missed."],

 ["nutrition","Which nutrient is most commonly deficient in landscape soils?","Nitrogen. Phosphorus is rarely deficient in established landscapes, despite being in nearly every bag sold."],
 ["nutrition","Old leaves show symptoms first — mobile or immobile nutrient?","Mobile. The tree relocates nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium and magnesium out of old tissue into new growth, so deficiency shows on older leaves."],
 ["nutrition","Interveinal chlorosis on the newest leaves points to what?","Iron or manganese, which are immobile. In most landscapes it reflects high soil pH limiting availability rather than an actual shortage in the soil."],
 ["nutrition","What is a typical annual maintenance nitrogen rate?","Roughly 2–4 lb of actual nitrogen per 1,000 sq ft of root zone per year, applying no more than about 1 lb of readily available N per 1,000 sq ft at a time."],
 ["nutrition","What must you do before writing a fertilizer prescription?","Test. A soil test gives pH, organic matter and nutrient levels; foliar analysis shows what the tree actually took up."],
 ["nutrition","Name four things fertilizer will not fix.","Compaction, girdling roots, incorrect planting depth, and borer infestation. It is the most commonly mis-sold treatment in the industry."],

 ["protection","How is the critical root zone commonly estimated?","A radius of about 1 ft per inch of trunk diameter, with tree protection zones often specified at 1 to 1.5 ft of radius per inch of DBH."],
 ["protection","Name the three ways construction damages trees.","Root severance and crushing, soil compaction from traffic and storage, and grade change over the root zone."],
 ["protection","What is allowed inside a tree protection zone?","Nothing. No traffic, storage, spoil, washout, fuel, or temporary structures — and the fence goes up before equipment arrives, not after."],
 ["protection","Trench through the root zone or bore beneath it?","Bore or tunnel beneath. Boring can preserve a tree that trenching would kill."],
 ["protection","How much fill over the root zone is dangerous?","Even 4 to 6 inches can be fatal on a sensitive species. Absorbing roots are in the top 6–12 inches and need oxygen."],
 ["protection","How soon does construction damage show up?","Typically on a three-to-seven-year lag, which is exactly why the contractor is never blamed."],

 ["urban","What is canopy cover and why does it matter?","The percentage of a land area under tree crowns from above. It is the headline metric communities set targets against and the strongest single predictor of urban forest benefit."],
 ["urban","What is i-Tree used for?","Quantifying urban forest structure and putting dollar values on stormwater interception, energy savings, air quality and carbon — the numbers that win budget votes."],
 ["urban","Why does age class distribution matter as much as species diversity?","A population planted all at once matures and dies all at once. A healthy urban forest spans young, establishing, mature and over-mature trees."],
 ["urban","What does a tree inventory change operationally?","It converts reactive emergency response into a planned pruning cycle, which costs substantially less per tree."],
 ["urban","Where is most of a city's canopy?","On private property in most American cities — which is why outreach and ordinance matter as much as the municipal planting budget."],

 ["support","Where on the stem is a support cable normally placed?","About two thirds of the distance from the defective union to the branch tips."],
 ["support","Static versus dynamic cable systems?","Static uses steel cable and rigid hardware to hold position. Dynamic uses hollow-braid synthetic rope installed with slack, permitting normal sway while limiting extremes so the tree keeps building reaction wood."],
 ["support","What should you consider before installing hardware?","End-weight reduction pruning. Shortening the lever arm is often more effective and much cheaper than a cable."],
 ["support","What obligation does installing a support system create?","A permanent, scheduled inspection duty — commonly annual — which must be documented and communicated to the owner in writing."],
 ["support","Is a cable an acceptable alternative to removing a structurally failing tree?","No. Hardware on a tree that should come down transfers liability rather than reducing risk."]
].map(function (r) { return { domain: r[0], q: r[1], a: r[2] }; });

